{"id":"gen-1790978346-rXLQZWW6imc6PsIfUZgo","object":"chat.completion","created":1790978346,"model":"cohere/north-mini-code:free","provider":"Cohere","system_fingerprint":null,"service_tier":null,"choices":[{"index":0,"logprobs":null,"finish_reason":"stop","native_finish_reason":"complete","message":{"role":"assistant","content":"{\n  \"intent\": \"PROPOSE\",\n  \"message_id\": \"csp-6412-c3-proposer-1\",\n  \"payload\": {\n    \"claim\": {\n      \"claim_id\": \"c3-tuples\",\n      \"dependencies\": [\n        {\n          \"claim_revision\": 1,\n          \"resource_id\": \"problem:csp-6412\"\n        }\n      ],\n      \"provenance\": {\n        \"message_id\": \"csp-6412-c3-proposer-1\"\n      },\n      \"revision\": 1,\n      \"state_revision\": 1,\n      \"status\": \"active\",\n      \"value\": {\n        \"component\": \"c3\",\n        \"tuples\": [\n          [\n            0,\n            2,\n            3,\n            0\n          ]\n        ],\n        \"variables\": [\n          \"i\",\n          \"j\",\n          \"k\",\n          \"l\"\n        ]\n      }\n    }\n  },\n  \"problem_id\": \"csp-6412\",\n  \"read_refs\": [\n    {\n      \"resource_id\": \"problem:csp-6412\",\n      \"revision\": 1,\n      \"state_revision\": 1\n    },\n    {\n      \"resource_id\": \"claim:c3-tuples\",\n      \"revision\": 0,\n      \"state_revision\": 0\n    }\n  ],\n  \"recipients\": [\n    \"c2-reviewer\",\n    \"shared-integrator\"\n  ],\n  \"reply_to\": null,\n  \"schema_version\": 1,\n  \"sender\": \"c3-proposer\"\n}","refusal":null,"reasoning":"We need to solve the finite constraint satisfaction problem (CSP). The problem description includes components and constraints. We need to enumerate all satisfying assignments for component c3 (variables i, j, k, l). The constraints are:\n\n- c1: variables a,b allowed pairs: (1,0), (1,3), (0,2), (2,3), (0,1), (0,3)\n- c2: b,c allowed pairs: (0,3), (1,1), (1,3), (1,2), (3,3), (0,2)\n- c3: c,d allowed pairs: (3,1), (0,1), (0,2), (1,0), (1,3), (1,1)\n- c4: d,a allowed pairs: (1,1), (2,1), (1,2), (2,2), (2,0), (3,2)\n- c5: e,f allowed pairs: (2,2), (1,2), (3,1), (1,3), (1,0), (2,0)\n- c6: f,g allowed pairs: (2,2), (1,1), (0,0), (1,3), (0,2), (0,3)\n- c7: g,h allowed pairs: (2,0), (3,2), (1,1), (2,1), (1,3), (3,1)\n- c8: h,e allowed pairs: (0,2), (1,0), (1,3), (2,3), (3,0), (2,1)\n- c9: i,j allowed pairs: (0,2), (2,0), (0,3), (3,2), (2,1), (2,3)\n- c10: j,k allowed pairs: (2,3), (0,0), (1,1), (2,2), (1,3), (3,0)\n- c11: k,l allowed pairs: (3,0), (0,3), (1,2), (1,0), (3,1), (3,3)\n- c12: l,i allowed pairs: (0,0), (3,3), (2,1), (2,0), (0,3), (1,3)\n- c13: m,n allowed pairs: (3,2), (2,2), (3,3), (1,1), (0,2), (1,2)\n- c14: n,o allowed pairs: (2,3), (3,2), (1,0), (1,2), (0,3), (2,2)\n- c15: o,p allowed pairs: (3,3), (1,0), (0,0), (0,1), (1,3), (3,2)\n- c16: p,m allowed pairs: (3,3), (1,0), (0,2), (1,1), (0,1), (3,0)\n- c17: b,e allowed pairs: (0,2), (0,3)\n- c18: f,i allowed pairs: (2,0), (3,1)\n- c19: j,m allowed pairs: (2,3), (0,0)\n- c20: n,a allowed pairs: (2,1), (3,3)\n\nAlso there are bridges linking components: c17 from c1 to c2 linking b,e; c18 from c2 to c3 linking f,i; c19 from c3 to c4 linking j,m; c20 from c4 to c1 linking n,a.\n\nWe need to find all assignments for a through p satisfying all constraints. Then enumerate all satisfying assignments for component c3 (i,j,k,l) sorted lexicographically. The output JSON should contain the \"tuples\" array with each tuple being [i,j,k,l] values. The template shows one placeholder tuple [0,0,0,0] but we need to replace with correct list.\n\nWe need to solve the CSP. Let's proceed systematically.\n\nFirst, we have variables: a,b,c,d,e,f,g,h,i,j,k,l,m,n,o,p each domain {0,1,2,3}.\n\nConstraints:\n\nc1: (a,b) allowed pairs: (1,0), (1,3), (0,2), (2,3), (0,1), (0,3). So allowed (a,b) combos:\n\n- a=1,b=0\n- a=1,b=3\n- a=0,b=2\n- a=2,b=3\n- a=0,b=1\n- a=0,b=3\n\nc2: (b,c) allowed pairs: (0,3), (1,1), (1,3), (1,2), (3,3), (0,2)\n\nc3: (c,d) allowed pairs: (3,1), (0,1), (0,2), (1,0), (1,3), (1,1)\n\nc4: (d,a) allowed pairs: (1,1), (2,1), (1,2), (2,2), (2,0), (3,2)\n\nc5: (e,f) allowed pairs: (2,2), (1,2), (3,1), (1,3), (1,0), (2,0)\n\nc6: (f,g) allowed pairs: (2,2), (1,1), (0,0), (1,3), (0,2), (0,3)\n\nc7: (g,h) allowed pairs: (2,0), (3,2), (1,1), (2,1), (1,3), (3,1)\n\nc8: (h,e) allowed pairs: (0,2), (1,0), (1,3), (2,3), (3,0), (2,1)\n\nc9: (i,j) allowed pairs: (0,2), (2,0), (0,3), (3,2), (2,1), (2,3)\n\nc10: (j,k) allowed pairs: (2,3), (0,0), (1,1), (2,2), (1,3), (3,0)\n\nc11: (k,l) allowed pairs: (3,0), (0,3), (1,2), (1,0), (3,1), (3,3)\n\nc12: (l,i) allowed pairs: (0,0), (3,3), (2,1), (2,0), (0,3), (1,3)\n\nc13: (m,n) allowed pairs: (3,2), (2,2), (3,3), (1,1), (0,2), (1,2)\n\nc14: (n,o) allowed pairs: (2,3), (3,2), (1,0), (1,2), (0,3), (2,2)\n\nc15: (o,p) allowed pairs: (3,3), (1,0), (0,0), (0,1), (1,3), (3,2)\n\nc16: (p,m) allowed pairs: (3,3), (1,0), (0,2), (1,1), (0,1), (3,0)\n\nc17: (b,e) allowed pairs: (0,2), (0,3)\n\nc18: (f,i) allowed pairs: (2,0), (3,1)\n\nc19: (j,m) allowed pairs: (2,3), (0,0)\n\nc20: (n,a) allowed pairs: (2,1), (3,3)\n\nWe need to find all assignments for all 16 variables satisfying all constraints.\n\nWe need to enumerate all possible assignments for c3 (i,j,k,l). So we need to find all possible combos for i,j,k,l that can be part of a full solution.\n\nWe need to consider constraints linking c3 variables to other components: c9 (i,j) within c3; c10 (j,k); c11 (k,l); c12 (l,i) all within c3. So c3 constraints are all internal to c3. So any assignment to i,j,k,l must satisfy c9, c10, c11, c12.\n\nThus we can first enumerate all tuples (i,j,k,l) in domain {0,1,2,3}^4 that satisfy those four constraints.\n\nThen we need to check whether they can be extended to full solution with other variables. So we need to find all assignments for a,b,c,d,e,f,g,h,m,n,o,p that satisfy constraints linking to c3 via c18 (f,i), c19 (j,m). Also c17 (b,e) etc.\n\nThus we need to solve the CSP globally, then extract all c3 assignments that appear in any solution.\n\nWe must enumerate all satisfying assignments for c3 (i,j,k,l) across all solutions. The problem says \"Enumerate ALL and ONLY satisfying assignments for component c3, sorted lexicographically.\" So we need to list each distinct tuple (i,j,k,l) that can appear in at least one full solution. If a tuple appears in multiple solutions, we list it only once.\n\nThus we need to find the set of possible (i,j,k,l) combos.\n\nWe need to produce JSON with \"tuples\" array containing those combos in lexicographic order (presumably sorted by i then j then k then l). The domain values are integers.\n\nSo the task is to solve the CSP.\n\nApproach: brute force enumeration? There are 4^16 = 4,294,967,296 possibilities, too many. But we can prune heavily using constraints.\n\nWe can use constraint propagation manually. Let's try to solve systematically.\n\nWe have constraints in a cyclic structure: c1-c4 form a cycle a->b->c->d->a. c5-c8 form another cycle e->f->g->h->e. c9-c12 form third cycle i->j->k->l->i. c13-c16 form fourth cycle m->n->o->p->m. Then cross constraints: c17 (b,e), c18 (f,i), c19 (j,m), c20 (n,a). So we have four cycles interlinked.\n\nWe need to find all assignments.\n\nWe can approach by solving each cycle with constraints and then combine.\n\nBecause each cycle is relatively small (4 variables each). Let's start with c3 (i,j,k,l) constraints.\n\n### c3 constraints (i,j,k,l):\n\nc9: (i,j) allowed pairs: (0,2), (2,0), (0,3), (3,2), (2,1), (2,3)\n\nList all possible (i,j):\n\n- (0,2)\n- (2,0)\n- (0,3)\n- (3,2)\n- (2,1)\n- (2,3)\n\nc10: (j,k) allowed pairs: (2,3), (0,0), (1,1), (2,2), (1,3), (3,0)\n\nc11: (k,l) allowed pairs: (3,0), (0,3), (1,2), (1,0), (3,1), (3,3)\n\nc12: (l,i) allowed pairs: (0,0), (3,3), (2,1), (2,0), (0,3), (1,3)\n\nThus we need to find all (i,j,k,l) combos satisfying all four.\n\nWe can enumerate possibilities for i and j from c9, then for each possible j find k from c10, then for each k find l from c11, then check l,i from c12.\n\nLet's enumerate systematically.\n\n**Step 1: enumerate i,j pairs**:\n\nList all (i,j) from c9:\n\n1. (i=0, j=2)\n2. (i=2, j=0)\n3. (i=0, j=3)\n4. (i=3, j=2)\n5. (i=2, j=1)\n6. (i=2, j=3)\n\nNow for each, find possible k from c10 given j.\n\nc10 allowed (j,k):\n- (2,3)\n- (0,0)\n- (1,1)\n- (2,2)\n- (1,3)\n- (3,0)\n\nThus for each j, possible k values:\n\n- If j=2: allowed k = 3 (from (2,3)) only? Also check if any other pair with j=2? The list includes (2,3) only. So k=3.\n\n- If j=0: allowed k = 0 (from (0,0)) only? Also any (j=0,k?) There's (0,0) only. So k=0.\n\n- If j=3: allowed k = 0 (from (3,0)) only? Also (1,3) has j=1, not j=3. So only k=0.\n\n- If j=1: allowed k = 1 (from (1,1)) only? Also (1,3) has j=1, k=3. So for j=1, k can be 1 or 3.\n\n- If j=2: we already covered.\n\n- If j=3: we covered.\n\nThus for each (i,j) we have:\n\n1. (i=0, j=2) => k=3 only.\n2. (i=2, j=0) => k=0 only.\n3. (i=0, j=3) => k=0 only.\n4. (i=3, j=2) => k=3 only.\n5. (i=2, j=1) => k=1 or 3.\n6. (i=2, j=3) => k=0 only.\n\nNow for each (i,j,k) we need to find possible l from c11 (k,l). c11 allowed (k,l) pairs:\n\n- (3,0)\n- (0,3)\n- (1,2)\n- (1,0)\n- (3,1)\n- (3,3)\n\nThus for each k, possible l values:\n\n- If k=3: l can be 0,1,3 (from (3,0), (3,1), (3,3)). Also (3,?) any other? (3,?) only those three.\n\n- If k=0: l can be 3 (from (0,3)). Only.\n\n- If k=1: l can be 2 (from (1,2)) or 0 (from (1,0)). So l=2 or 0.\n\n- If k=2: not present? Actually there is no (2,l) in c11. So k=2 is invalid.\n\nThus for each case, we need to consider l possibilities.\n\nNow after we have (i,j,k,l), we must satisfy c12: (l,i) allowed pairs: (0,0), (3,3), (2,1), (2,0), (0,3), (1,3).\n\nThus for each (i,l) we need to check if (l,i) is in that list. Actually c12 constraint is (l,i) allowed pairs: the pairs listed are (l,i) combos. So we need (l,i) to be one of those.\n\nThus we need to check each candidate.\n\nLet's enumerate each case.\n\n**Case 1:** (i=0, j=2, k=3). l possibilities: 0,1,3.\n\n- (i=0,l=0): Check (l,i) = (0,0) is allowed? Yes, (0,0) is in c12 list. So (0,0) allowed. So (i,j,k,l) = (0,2,3,0) is valid.\n\n- (i=0,l=1): (l,i) = (1,0). Is (1,0) allowed? c12 list includes (0,0), (3,3), (2,1), (2,0), (0,3), (1,3). (1,0) is not present. So invalid.\n\n- (i=0,l=3): (l,i) = (3,0). Is (3,0) allowed? Not in list. The list includes (0,3) but not (3,0). So invalid.\n\nThus only (0,2,3,0) from case 1.\n\n**Case 2:** (i=2, j=0, k=0). l possibilities: only l=3 (since k=0 => l=3). So (i=2,j=0,k=0,l=3). Check (l,i) = (3,2). Is (3,2) allowed? c12 list includes (0,0), (3,3), (2,1), (2,0), (0,3), (1,3). (3,2) not present. So invalid. So case 2 yields no solution.\n\n**Case 3:** (i=0, j=3, k=0). l=3. So (i=0,j=3,k=0,l=3). Check (l,i) = (3,0). Not allowed. So invalid.\n\n**Case 4:** (i=3, j=2, k=3). l possibilities: 0,1,3.\n\n- (i=3,l=0): (l,i) = (0,3). Is (0,3) allowed? Yes, c12 includes (0,3). So (3,2,3,0) valid.\n\n- (i=3,l=1): (1,3) allowed? c12 includes (1,3). Yes. So (3,2,3,1) valid.\n\n- (i=3,l=3): (3,3) allowed? Yes, c12 includes (3,3). So (3,2,3,3) valid.\n\nThus three solutions from case 4: (3,2,3,0), (3,2,3,1), (3,2,3,3).\n\n**Case 5:** (i=2, j=1, k=1 or 3). Let's branch.\n\n- Subcase 5a: k=1. Then l possibilities: from k=1 => l=2 or 0.\n\n-- (i=2,j=1,k=1,l=2). Check (l,i) = (2,2). Is (2,2) allowed? c12 list includes (0,0), (3,3), (2,1), (2,0), (0,3), (1,3). (2,2) not present. So invalid.\n\n-- (i=2,j=1,k=1,l=0). (l,i) = (0,2). Is (0,2) allowed? Not in c12 list. So invalid.\n\nThus k=1 yields no solutions.\n\n- Subcase 5b: k=3. Then l possibilities: 0,1,3.\n\n-- (i=2,j=1,k=3,l=0). (l,i) = (0,2). Not allowed.\n\n-- (i=2,j=1,k=3,l=1). (l,i) = (1,2). Not allowed.\n\n-- (i=2,j=1,k=3,l=3). (l,i) = (3,2). Not allowed.\n\nThus case 5 yields no solutions.\n\n**Case 6:** (i=2, j=3, k=0). l=3. So (i=2,j=3,k=0,l=3). Check (l,i) = (3,2). Not allowed. So invalid.\n\nThus the only valid (i,j,k,l) combos are:\n\n1. (0,2,3,0)\n2. (3,2,3,0)\n3. (3,2,3,1)\n4. (3,2,3,3)\n\nSo we have 4 possible tuples for c3.\n\nBut we need to ensure these can be extended to a full solution. There may be further constraints linking i to f (c18) and j to m (c19). So we need to see if for each of these tuples, we can assign values to other variables satisfying all constraints.\n\nThus we need to find assignments for a,b,c,d,e,f,g,h,m,n,o,p such that:\n\n- (i,j,k,l) is one of the above.\n- c18: (f,i) allowed pairs: (2,0), (3,1). So (f,i) must be either (2,0) or (3,1). So for each i, we need to see possible f values.\n\n- c19: (j,m) allowed pairs: (2,3), (0,0). So (j,m) must be either (2,3) or (0,0). So for each j, possible m.\n\n- c17: (b,e) allowed pairs: (0,2), (0,3). So b must be 0 and e must be 2 or 3.\n\n- c20: (n,a) allowed pairs: (2,1), (3,3). So (n,a) must be either (2,1) or (3,3).\n\nThus we need to find assignments for a,b,c,d,e,f,g,h,m,n,o,p that satisfy all constraints in cycles c1-c4, c5-c8, c13-c16, plus cross constraints.\n\nGiven the limited possibilities for b,e and n,a, we can try to solve.\n\nLet's treat each cycle separately.\n\n### Cycle a,b,c,d (c1-c4)\n\nVariables: a,b,c,d.\n\nConstraints:\n- c1: (a,b) allowed pairs: (1,0), (1,3), (0,2), (2,3), (0,1), (0,3)\n- c2: (b,c) allowed pairs: (0,3), (1,1), (1,3), (1,2), (3,3), (0,2)\n- c3: (c,d) allowed pairs: (3,1), (0,1), (0,2), (1,0), (1,3), (1,1)\n- c4: (d,a) allowed pairs: (1,1), (2,1), (1,2), (2,2), (2,0), (3,2)\n\nWe also have cross constraints: c17: (b,e) allowed (0,2) or (0,3) => b=0, e=2 or 3. So b must be 0. Also c20: (n,a) allowed (2,1) or (3,3) => a must be 1 if n=2, or a=3 if n=3. So a is either 1 or 3.\n\nThus b=0 is forced by c17. Let's check if any other constraints allow b=0. c1 and c2 must be satisfied with b=0.\n\nNow c17 also forces e=2 or 3.\n\nThus we can start solving the a,b,c,d cycle with b=0, a∈{1,3} (due to n constraints). Let's see.\n\nGiven b=0, we need to find possible a from c1: allowed (a,b) pairs include (1,0), (0,2) but b=0, so possible pairs with b=0 are:\n\n- (a=1,b=0) is allowed.\n- (a=0,b=2) not applicable because b=0.\n- (a=2,b=3) no.\n- (a=0,b=1) no.\n- (a=0,b=3) no.\n- (a=1,b=3) no.\n\nThus only (a=1,b=0) is allowed. So a must be 1. That matches c20: (n,a) allowed (2,1) or (3,3). So a=1 is allowed only with n=2. So n must be 2.\n\nThus a=1, b=0, n=2.\n\nThus c20 satisfied: (n=2,a=1) is allowed (2,1) pair.\n\nThus we have a=1, b=0, n=2.\n\nNow we need to find c,d satisfying c2 and c3 and c4.\n\nGiven b=0, c2: (b,c) allowed pairs: (0,3) and (0,2) are the only ones with b=0? Let's list c2 allowed pairs: (0,3), (1,1), (1,3), (1,2), (3,3), (0,2). So with b=0, possible c values: c=3 (pair (0,3)) or c=2 (pair (0,2)). So c ∈ {2,3}.\n\nNow c3: (c,d) allowed pairs: (3,1), (0,1), (0,2), (1,0), (1,3), (1,1). So for each possible c, we need to find d.\n\n- If c=2: does any allowed pair have c=2? Let's see: (0,2) has c=0, not 2. (1,2) not present. (2,?) none. Actually c3 list includes (0,2) meaning (c=0,d=2). No pair with c=2. So c cannot be 2. So c must be 3.\n\nThus c=3.\n\nNow c3 with c=3: allowed pairs where c=3? c3 list: (3,1) is (c=3,d=1). That's the only one with c=3. So d=1.\n\nThus d=1.\n\nNow check c4: (d,a) allowed pairs: (1,1), (2,1), (1,2), (2,2), (2,0), (3,2). Here d=1, a=1 => (1,1) is allowed. So c4 satisfied.\n\nThus a,b,c,d solved uniquely: a=1,b=0,c=3,d=1.\n\nThus the a,b,c,d cycle is uniquely determined given cross constraints.\n\nThus we have a=1,b=0,c=3,d=1,n=2.\n\nNow we have constraints for e,f,g,h (c5-c8) with cross constraints c17 (b,e) and c8 (h,e) and c5 (e,f), c6 (f,g), c7 (g,h). Also c18 (f,i) linking to i.\n\nWe also have constraints for m,n,o,p (c13-c16) with cross constraints c19 (j,m) and c20 (n,a) already satisfied, c16 (p,m) linking to m, etc.\n\nThus we need to solve e,f,g,h cycle and m,n,o,p cycle.\n\nLet's start with e,f,g,h.\n\n### Variables e,f,g,h\n\nConstraints:\n\n- c5: (e,f) allowed pairs: (2,2), (1,2), (3,1), (1,3), (1,0), (2,0)\n- c6: (f,g) allowed pairs: (2,2), (1,1), (0,0), (1,3), (0,2), (0,3)\n- c7: (g,h) allowed pairs: (2,0), (3,2), (1,1), (2,1), (1,3), (3,1)\n- c8: (h,e) allowed pairs: (0,2), (1,0), (1,3), (2,3), (3,0), (2,1)\n\nCross constraints:\n\n- c17: (b,e) allowed pairs: (0,2), (0,3). Since b=0, e must be 2 or 3.\n\nThus e ∈ {2,3}.\n\nAlso c18: (f,i) allowed pairs: (2,0), (3,1). So for each i (from c3 tuple), f must be either 2 if i=0, or 3 if i=1. Since i can be 0 or 3 (looking at our c3 solutions: i values are 0 (first tuple) or 3 (others)). So:\n\n- If i=0 => f must be 2 (since (2,0) is allowed).\n- If i=3 => f must be 3 (since (3,1) is allowed). Note (3,1) means f=3,i=1? Wait c18 allowed pairs: (2,0) and (3,1). The pair is (f,i). So (f=2,i=0) or (f=3,i=1). So if i=0, f=2. If i=1, f=3. But i values are 0 or 3. So i=0 => f=2. i=3 => no allowed pair because (f,i) must be (2,0) or (3,1). There's no (f,i) with i=3. So any solution with i=3 is impossible because c18 cannot be satisfied. Wait check: c18 allowed pairs: (2,0) and (3,1). So i must be 0 or 1. But our c3 solutions have i values: 0,3,3,3. So only the tuple with i=0 is possible: (0,2,3,0). That has i=0. The other three tuples have i=3, which cannot satisfy c18. Therefore only the first tuple (i=0,j=2,k=3,l=0) can be part of a full solution. So we can prune other three c3 tuples.\n\nThus the only viable c3 tuple is (i=0, j=2, k=3, l=0). So we will have i=0, j=2, k=3, l=0.\n\nThus we have cross constraints:\n\n- c18: (f,i) => (f,0) must be (2,0) => f=2.\n\n- c19: (j,m) => (2,m) must be either (2,3) or (0,0). Since j=2, allowed pairs: (2,3) or (0,0). So (j,m) = (2,3) => m=3. Or (j,m) = (0,0) is impossible because j=2 not 0. So m must be 3.\n\nThus m=3.\n\nThus we have determined f=2, m=3.\n\nNow we need to solve e,f,g,h cycle with e ∈ {2,3} (from c17) and f=2 (from above). So e must be such that (e,f) allowed in c5: (e,2) must be in allowed pairs for c5.\n\nc5 allowed (e,f): (2,2), (1,2), (3,1), (1,3), (1,0), (2,0). So (e,f) pairs where f=2 are:\n\n- (2,2) allowed: e=2, f=2.\n- (1,2) allowed: e=1, f=2.\n- (2,0) has f=0, not relevant.\n- (3,1) f=1.\n- (1,3) f=3.\n- (1,0) f=0.\n\nThus with f=2, possible e values are 2 (via (2,2)) or 1 (via (1,2)). But e must be 2 or 3 from c17. So e=2 is allowed; e=1 is not allowed. So e must be 2.\n\nThus e=2.\n\nNow we have e=2, f=2.\n\nNow c6: (f,g) => (2,g) must be allowed. c6 allowed pairs: (2,2), (1,1), (0,0), (1,3), (0,2), (0,3). So with f=2, possible g values: g=2 (pair (2,2)). Also any other? No, only (2,2). So g=2.\n\nThus g=2.\n\nNow c7: (g,h) => (2,h) must be allowed. c7 allowed pairs: (2,0), (3,2), (1,1), (2,1), (1,3), (3,1). So with g=2, possible h values: h=0 (pair (2,0)) or h=1 (pair (2,1)). So h ∈ {0,1}.\n\nNow c8: (h,e) => (h,2) must be allowed. c8 allowed pairs: (0,2), (1,0), (1,3), (2,3), (3,0), (2,1). So we need (h,2) to be one of those. Let's list allowed (h,e) pairs:\n\n- (0,2) => h=0, e=2 allowed.\n- (1,0) => h=1, e=0 not relevant (e=2).\n- (1,3) => h=1, e=3 not relevant.\n- (2,3) => h=2, e=3 not relevant.\n- (3,0) => h=3, e=0 not relevant.\n- (2,1) => h=2, e=1 not relevant.\n\nThus only (0,2) works for e=2. So h must be 0.\n\nThus h=0.\n\nThus we have e=2,f=2,g=2,h=0.\n\nNow check all constraints for e,f,g,h:\n\n- c5 (e,f) = (2,2) allowed.\n- c6 (f,g) = (2,2) allowed.\n- c7 (g,h) = (2,0) allowed.\n- c8 (h,e) = (0,2) allowed.\n- c17 (b,e) = (0,2) allowed.\n\nThus e,f,g,h cycle solved uniquely: e=2,f=2,g=2,h=0.\n\nThus we have solved all variables except m,n,o,p.\n\nWe have determined:\n\n- a=1\n- b=0\n- c=3\n- d=1\n- e=2\n- f=2\n- g=2\n- h=0\n- i=0\n- j=2\n- k=3\n- l=0\n- m=3 (from j,m)\n- n=2 (from a,n)\n- o and p unknown (to be solved)\n- p unknown\n\nNow we need to solve m,n,o,p cycle (c13-c16) with constraints:\n\nc13: (m,n) allowed pairs: (3,2), (2,2), (3,3), (1,1), (0,2), (1,2)\nc14: (n,o) allowed pairs: (2,3), (3,2), (1,0), (1,2), (0,3), (2,2)\nc15: (o,p) allowed pairs: (3,3), (1,0), (0,0), (0,1), (1,3), (3,2)\nc16: (p,m) allowed pairs: (3,3), (1,0), (0,2), (1,1), (0,1), (3,0)\n\nWe have m=3, n=2 (from earlier). Let's verify that (m,n) = (3,2) is allowed: c13 includes (3,2) yes. So (m=3,n=2) satisfies c13.\n\nNow we need to find o and p such that:\n\n- c14: (n,o) = (2,o) must be allowed. c14 allowed pairs: (2,3), (3,2), (1,0), (1,2), (0,3), (2,2). So with n=2, allowed o values: o=3 (pair (2,3)), or o=2 (pair (2,2)). So o ∈ {2,3}.\n\n- c15: (o,p) must be allowed.\n\n- c16: (p,m) = (p,3) must be allowed. c16 allowed pairs: (3,3), (1,0), (0,2), (1,1), (0,1), (3,0). So with m=3, we need (p,3) to be one of those. Let's list allowed (p,m) pairs:\n\n-- (3,3): p=3,m=3 allowed.\n-- (1,0): p=1,m=0 not relevant.\n-- (0,2): p=0,m=2 not relevant.\n-- (1,1): p=1,m=1 not relevant.\n-- (0,1): p=0,m=1 not relevant.\n-- (3,0): p=3,m=0 not relevant.\n\nThus only (p=3,m=3) works for m=3. So p must be 3.\n\nThus p=3.\n\nNow c16 satisfied: (p,m) = (3,3) is allowed.\n\nNow c15: (o,p) = (o,3) must be allowed. c15 allowed pairs: (3,3), (1,0), (0,0), (0,1), (1,3), (3,2). So (o,3) allowed if:\n\n- (3,3) => o=3.\n- (1,3) => o=1.\n- (3,2) => o=3? Actually (3,2) is (o=3,p=2) not relevant.\n- (0,1) => p=1 not relevant.\n- (0,0) => p=0 not relevant.\n- (1,0) => p=0 not relevant.\n\nThus allowed (o,3) combos: o=3 (via (3,3)), or o=1 (via (1,3)). So o ∈ {1,3}.\n\nBut earlier from c14, o ∈ {2,3}. Intersection yields o=3 only (since 1 not allowed). So o=3.\n\nThus o=3.\n\nNow check c14: (n,o) = (2,3) is allowed (c14 includes (2,3)). Good.\n\nNow c15: (o,p) = (3,3) is allowed (c15 includes (3,3)). Good.\n\nThus the m,n,o,p cycle solved uniquely: m=3,n=2,o=3,p=3.\n\nThus we have a full assignment for all variables:\n\n- a=1\n- b=0\n- c=3\n- d=1\n- e=2\n- f=2\n- g=2\n- h=0\n- i=0\n- j=2\n- k=3\n- l=0\n- m=3\n- n=2\n- o=3\n- p=3\n\nWe need to verify all constraints are satisfied. Let's double-check each constraint:\n\nc1 (a,b) = (1,0) allowed? Yes (1,0) is in c1 allowed pairs.\n\nc2 (b,c) = (0,3) allowed? Yes (0,3) is in c2.\n\nc3 (c,d) = (3,1) allowed? Yes (3,1) is in c3.\n\nc4 (d,a) = (1,1) allowed? Yes (1,1) is in c4.\n\nc5 (e,f) = (2,2) allowed? Yes (2,2) is in c5.\n\nc6 (f,g) = (2,2) allowed? Yes (2,2) is in c6.\n\nc7 (g,h) = (2,0) allowed? Yes (2,0) is in c7.\n\nc8 (h,e) = (0,2) allowed? Yes (0,2) is in c8.\n\nc9 (i,j) = (0,2) allowed? Yes (0,2) is in c9.\n\nc10 (j,k) = (2,3) allowed? Yes (2,3) is in c10.\n\nc11 (k,l) = (3,0) allowed? Yes (3,0) is in c11.\n\nc12 (l,i) = (0,0) allowed? Yes (0,0) is in c12.\n\nc13 (m,n) = (3,2) allowed? Yes (3,2) is in c13.\n\nc14 (n,o) = (2,3) allowed? Yes (2,3) is in c14.\n\nc15 (o,p) = (3,3) allowed? Yes (3,3) is in c15.\n\nc16 (p,m) = (3,3) allowed? Yes (3,3) is in c16.\n\nc17 (b,e) = (0,2) allowed? Yes (0,2) is in c17.\n\nc18 (f,i) = (2,0) allowed? Yes (2,0) is in c18.\n\nc19 (j,m) = (2,3) allowed? Yes (2,3) is in c19.\n\nc20 (n,a) = (2,1) allowed? Yes (2,1) is in c20.\n\nAll constraints satisfied. So we have at least one full solution.\n\nNow we need to enumerate all satisfying assignments for component c3 (i,j,k,l). We found only one tuple (0,2,3,0) appears in this solution. But there might be other full solutions with different assignments for other variables that still satisfy constraints but produce other c3 tuples. However earlier we reasoned that only c3 tuple (0,2,3,0) is possible because c18 restricts i to 0 or 1. Our c3 tuple possibilities included i=0 and i=3. Since c18 only allows i=0 (with f=2) or i=1 (with f=3). There is no i=3 allowed. So any solution must have i=0 (since i=0 is allowed) or i=1 (but we have no tuple with i=1). So i must be 0. Thus only c3 tuple with i=0 is viable. That is (0,2,3,0). So indeed only that tuple can appear in any solution.\n\nBut we need to be thorough: Are there any other c3 tuples with i=0? Our enumeration gave only (0,2,3,0). Let's double-check enumeration for c3 to ensure we didn't miss any other (i,j,k,l) combos with i=0.\n\nWe enumerated all possible (i,j) from c9. For i=0, possible j values: from c9 allowed pairs with i=0: (0,2) and (0,3). So i=0, j=2 or j=3.\n\nWe considered both:\n\n- i=0,j=2 => k=3 => l possibilities: 0,1,3 => only l=0 works (due to c12). So (0,2,3,0) is valid.\n\n- i=0,j=3 => k=0 => l=3 => (0,3,0,3) fails c12. So no other.\n\nThus only (0,2,3,0) for i=0.\n\nThus only one c3 tuple possible.\n\nThus the answer should be a JSON with \"tuples\": [[0,2,3,0]].\n\nBut we need to verify if there could be any alternative assignments for other variables that allow i=1? Let's check if any c3 tuple has i=1. Our enumeration didn't include any because c9 allowed i values: from c9 allowed pairs: (0,2), (2,0), (0,3), (3,2), (2,1), (2,3). So i can be 0,2,3. No i=1. So i cannot be 1. So c18 cannot be satisfied if i=1, but there is no i=1 anyway. So i must be 0 or 2 or 3. But c18 only allows i=0 (with f=2) or i=1 (with f=3). So i=2 or 3 are not allowed. Therefore only i=0 is possible. So indeed only the tuple with i=0 is viable.\n\nThus the only c3 tuple is (0,2,3,0). So the answer is just that tuple.\n\nBut we need to double-check that there is no alternative assignment for other variables that could allow i=0 but with different j,k,l? Our enumeration for c3 gave only one tuple with i=0. So no alternative.\n\nThus answer is a JSON with \"tuples\": [[0,2,3,0]].\n\nBut we need to ensure the JSON format matches the template. The template includes fields:\n\n{\n  \"intent\": \"PROPOSE\",\n  \"message_id\": \"csp-6412-c3-proposer-1\",\n  \"payload\": {\n    \"claim\": {\n      \"claim_id\": \"c3-tuples\",\n      \"dependencies\": [\n        {\n          \"claim_revision\": 1,\n          \"resource_id\": \"problem:csp-6412\"\n        }\n      ],\n      \"provenance\": {\n        \"message_id\": \"csp-6412-c3-proposer-1\"\n      },\n      \"revision\": 1,\n      \"state_revision\": 1,\n      \"status\": \"active\",\n      \"value\": {\n        \"component\": \"c3\",\n        \"tuples\": [\n          [\n            0,\n            0,\n            0,\n            0\n          ]\n        ],\n        \"variables\": [\n          \"i\",\n          \"j\",\n          \"k\",\n          \"l\"\n        ]\n      }\n    }\n  },\n  \"problem_id\": \"csp-6412\",\n  \"read_refs\": [\n    {\n      \"resource_id\": \"problem:csp-6412\",\n      \"revision\": 1,\n      \"state_revision\": 1\n    },\n    {\n      \"resource_id\": \"claim:c3-tuples\",\n      \"revision\": 0,\n      \"state_revision\": 0\n    }\n  ],\n  \"recipients\": [\n    \"c2-reviewer\",\n    \"shared-integrator\"\n  ],\n  \"reply_to\": null,\n  \"schema_version\": 1,\n  \"sender\": \"c3-proposer\"\n}\n\nWe need to edit only payload.claim.value.tuples. So we need to replace the placeholder tuple with the correct tuple(s). The \"tuples\" array should contain all satisfying tuples for component c3. Since we have only one, it should be [[0,2,3,0]].\n\nThus we need to output JSON exactly as the template but with that change. The \"variables\" list remains [\"i\",\"j\",\"k\",\"l\"].\n\nThus final JSON:\n\n{\n  \"intent\": \"PROPOSE\",\n  \"message_id\": \"csp-6412-c3-proposer-1\",\n  \"payload\": {\n    \"claim\": {\n      \"claim_id\": \"c3-tuples\",\n      \"dependencies\": [\n        {\n          \"claim_revision\": 1,\n          \"resource_id\": \"problem:csp-6412\"\n        }\n      ],\n      \"provenance\": {\n        \"message_id\": \"csp-6412-c3-proposer-1\"\n      },\n      \"revision\": 1,\n      \"state_revision\": 1,\n      \"status\": \"active\",\n      \"value\": {\n        \"component\": \"c3\",\n        \"tuples\": [\n          [\n            0,\n            2,\n            3,\n            0\n          ]\n        ],\n        \"variables\": [\n          \"i\",\n          \"j\",\n          \"k\",\n          \"l\"\n        ]\n      }\n    }\n  },\n  \"problem_id\": \"csp-6412\",\n  \"read_refs\": [\n    {\n      \"resource_id\": \"problem:csp-6412\",\n      \"revision\": 1,\n      \"state_revision\": 1\n    },\n    {\n      \"resource_id\": \"claim:c3-tuples\",\n      \"revision\": 0,\n      \"state_revision\": 0\n    }\n  ],\n  \"recipients\": [\n    \"c2-reviewer\",\n    \"shared-integrator\"\n  ],\n  \"reply_to\": null,\n  \"schema_version\": 1,\n  \"sender\": \"c3-proposer\"\n}\n\nWe must ensure no extra whitespace or formatting beyond JSON. But it's okay to have newlines and indentation; it's still valid JSON. The requirement says \"Return exactly one JSON protocol envelope matching the supplied template; no prose outside JSON. Do not wrap in markdown code blocks or backticks. Output raw unformatted JSON starting with { and ending with }.\" So we can output the JSON with pretty formatting (newlines). That's fine.\n\nThus final answer is the JSON above.\n\nBut we need to double-check that there are no other solutions for c3 that we missed due to possible alternative assignments for other variables that could allow i=2 or i=3 with different f values? c18 only allows (f,i) pairs (2,0) and (3,1). So i can be 0 or 1. So i cannot be 2 or 3. So indeed only i=0 is possible. So only tuple with i=0 is possible. So we are done.\n\nThus answer is as above.\n\nBut before finalizing, we should double-check that there is no alternative assignment for a,b,c,d that could allow b not equal to 0, maybe c17 could be satisfied with b=0 only, but maybe there is alternative for c17? c17 allowed pairs: (0,2) and (0,3). So b must be 0. So b=0 forced. So a must be 1 as we found. So a=1 forced. So n must be 2 (since (n,a) allowed (2,1) or (3,3)). So n=2 forced. So m must be 3 (since (j,m) allowed (2,3) or (0,0) and j=2). So m=3 forced. So e must be 2 (since (b,e) allowed (0,2) or (0,3) and (e,f) allowed only e=2 given f=2). So e=2 forced. So f=2 forced. So g=2 forced. So h=0 forced. So all variables forced uniquely. So the solution is unique. So indeed only one full solution exists, and thus only one c3 tuple.\n\nThus answer is correct.\n\nNow we need to output JSON with the tuple.\n\nOne more check: Are there any constraints that could be satisfied by alternative values for e? Let's see: c17 (b,e) allowed (0,2) or (0,3). So e could be 2 or 3. However c5 (e,f) allowed (e,2) pairs: (2,2) and (1,2). So if e=3, then (e,f) = (3,2) is not allowed. So e cannot be 3. So e=2 forced. So indeed unique.\n\nThus answer is final.\n\nNow we need to produce JSON exactly as described.\n\nWe must ensure that the \"tuples\" array contains the tuple with correct ordering: [i,j,k,l] = [0,2,3,0].\n\nThus final answer.\n\nWe should ensure no extra keys or modifications. The template includes \"read_refs\" referencing claim:c3-tuples with revision 0, state_revision 0. That is fine.\n\nThus final answer is as above.\n\nNow we output the JSON.","reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.text","text":"We need to solve the finite constraint satisfaction problem (CSP). The problem description includes components and constraints. We need to enumerate all satisfying assignments for component c3 (variables i, j, k, l). The constraints are:\n\n- c1: variables a,b allowed pairs: (1,0), (1,3), (0,2), (2,3), (0,1), (0,3)\n- c2: b,c allowed pairs: (0,3), (1,1), (1,3), (1,2), (3,3), (0,2)\n- c3: c,d allowed pairs: (3,1), (0,1), (0,2), (1,0), (1,3), (1,1)\n- c4: d,a allowed pairs: (1,1), (2,1), (1,2), (2,2), (2,0), (3,2)\n- c5: e,f allowed pairs: (2,2), (1,2), (3,1), (1,3), (1,0), (2,0)\n- c6: f,g allowed pairs: (2,2), (1,1), (0,0), (1,3), (0,2), (0,3)\n- c7: g,h allowed pairs: (2,0), (3,2), (1,1), (2,1), (1,3), (3,1)\n- c8: h,e allowed pairs: (0,2), (1,0), (1,3), (2,3), (3,0), (2,1)\n- c9: i,j allowed pairs: (0,2), (2,0), (0,3), (3,2), (2,1), (2,3)\n- c10: j,k allowed pairs: (2,3), (0,0), (1,1), (2,2), (1,3), (3,0)\n- c11: k,l allowed pairs: (3,0), (0,3), (1,2), (1,0), (3,1), (3,3)\n- c12: l,i allowed pairs: (0,0), (3,3), (2,1), (2,0), (0,3), (1,3)\n- c13: m,n allowed pairs: (3,2), (2,2), (3,3), (1,1), (0,2), (1,2)\n- c14: n,o allowed pairs: (2,3), (3,2), (1,0), (1,2), (0,3), (2,2)\n- c15: o,p allowed pairs: (3,3), (1,0), (0,0), (0,1), (1,3), (3,2)\n- c16: p,m allowed pairs: (3,3), (1,0), (0,2), (1,1), (0,1), (3,0)\n- c17: b,e allowed pairs: (0,2), (0,3)\n- c18: f,i allowed pairs: (2,0), (3,1)\n- c19: j,m allowed pairs: (2,3), (0,0)\n- c20: n,a allowed pairs: (2,1), (3,3)\n\nAlso there are bridges linking components: c17 from c1 to c2 linking b,e; c18 from c2 to c3 linking f,i; c19 from c3 to c4 linking j,m; c20 from c4 to c1 linking n,a.\n\nWe need to find all assignments for a through p satisfying all constraints. Then enumerate all satisfying assignments for component c3 (i,j,k,l) sorted lexicographically. The output JSON should contain the \"tuples\" array with each tuple being [i,j,k,l] values. The template shows one placeholder tuple [0,0,0,0] but we need to replace with correct list.\n\nWe need to solve the CSP. Let's proceed systematically.\n\nFirst, we have variables: a,b,c,d,e,f,g,h,i,j,k,l,m,n,o,p each domain {0,1,2,3}.\n\nConstraints:\n\nc1: (a,b) allowed pairs: (1,0), (1,3), (0,2), (2,3), (0,1), (0,3). So allowed (a,b) combos:\n\n- a=1,b=0\n- a=1,b=3\n- a=0,b=2\n- a=2,b=3\n- a=0,b=1\n- a=0,b=3\n\nc2: (b,c) allowed pairs: (0,3), (1,1), (1,3), (1,2), (3,3), (0,2)\n\nc3: (c,d) allowed pairs: (3,1), (0,1), (0,2), (1,0), (1,3), (1,1)\n\nc4: (d,a) allowed pairs: (1,1), (2,1), (1,2), (2,2), (2,0), (3,2)\n\nc5: (e,f) allowed pairs: (2,2), (1,2), (3,1), (1,3), (1,0), (2,0)\n\nc6: (f,g) allowed pairs: (2,2), (1,1), (0,0), (1,3), (0,2), (0,3)\n\nc7: (g,h) allowed pairs: (2,0), (3,2), (1,1), (2,1), (1,3), (3,1)\n\nc8: (h,e) allowed pairs: (0,2), (1,0), (1,3), (2,3), (3,0), (2,1)\n\nc9: (i,j) allowed pairs: (0,2), (2,0), (0,3), (3,2), (2,1), (2,3)\n\nc10: (j,k) allowed pairs: (2,3), (0,0), (1,1), (2,2), (1,3), (3,0)\n\nc11: (k,l) allowed pairs: (3,0), (0,3), (1,2), (1,0), (3,1), (3,3)\n\nc12: (l,i) allowed pairs: (0,0), (3,3), (2,1), (2,0), (0,3), (1,3)\n\nc13: (m,n) allowed pairs: (3,2), (2,2), (3,3), (1,1), (0,2), (1,2)\n\nc14: (n,o) allowed pairs: (2,3), (3,2), (1,0), (1,2), (0,3), (2,2)\n\nc15: (o,p) allowed pairs: (3,3), (1,0), (0,0), (0,1), (1,3), (3,2)\n\nc16: (p,m) allowed pairs: (3,3), (1,0), (0,2), (1,1), (0,1), (3,0)\n\nc17: (b,e) allowed pairs: (0,2), (0,3)\n\nc18: (f,i) allowed pairs: (2,0), (3,1)\n\nc19: (j,m) allowed pairs: (2,3), (0,0)\n\nc20: (n,a) allowed pairs: (2,1), (3,3)\n\nWe need to find all assignments for all 16 variables satisfying all constraints.\n\nWe need to enumerate all possible assignments for c3 (i,j,k,l). So we need to find all possible combos for i,j,k,l that can be part of a full solution.\n\nWe need to consider constraints linking c3 variables to other components: c9 (i,j) within c3; c10 (j,k); c11 (k,l); c12 (l,i) all within c3. So c3 constraints are all internal to c3. So any assignment to i,j,k,l must satisfy c9, c10, c11, c12.\n\nThus we can first enumerate all tuples (i,j,k,l) in domain {0,1,2,3}^4 that satisfy those four constraints.\n\nThen we need to check whether they can be extended to full solution with other variables. So we need to find all assignments for a,b,c,d,e,f,g,h,m,n,o,p that satisfy constraints linking to c3 via c18 (f,i), c19 (j,m). Also c17 (b,e) etc.\n\nThus we need to solve the CSP globally, then extract all c3 assignments that appear in any solution.\n\nWe must enumerate all satisfying assignments for c3 (i,j,k,l) across all solutions. The problem says \"Enumerate ALL and ONLY satisfying assignments for component c3, sorted lexicographically.\" So we need to list each distinct tuple (i,j,k,l) that can appear in at least one full solution. If a tuple appears in multiple solutions, we list it only once.\n\nThus we need to find the set of possible (i,j,k,l) combos.\n\nWe need to produce JSON with \"tuples\" array containing those combos in lexicographic order (presumably sorted by i then j then k then l). The domain values are integers.\n\nSo the task is to solve the CSP.\n\nApproach: brute force enumeration? There are 4^16 = 4,294,967,296 possibilities, too many. But we can prune heavily using constraints.\n\nWe can use constraint propagation manually. Let's try to solve systematically.\n\nWe have constraints in a cyclic structure: c1-c4 form a cycle a->b->c->d->a. c5-c8 form another cycle e->f->g->h->e. c9-c12 form third cycle i->j->k->l->i. c13-c16 form fourth cycle m->n->o->p->m. Then cross constraints: c17 (b,e), c18 (f,i), c19 (j,m), c20 (n,a). So we have four cycles interlinked.\n\nWe need to find all assignments.\n\nWe can approach by solving each cycle with constraints and then combine.\n\nBecause each cycle is relatively small (4 variables each). Let's start with c3 (i,j,k,l) constraints.\n\n### c3 constraints (i,j,k,l):\n\nc9: (i,j) allowed pairs: (0,2), (2,0), (0,3), (3,2), (2,1), (2,3)\n\nList all possible (i,j):\n\n- (0,2)\n- (2,0)\n- (0,3)\n- (3,2)\n- (2,1)\n- (2,3)\n\nc10: (j,k) allowed pairs: (2,3), (0,0), (1,1), (2,2), (1,3), (3,0)\n\nc11: (k,l) allowed pairs: (3,0), (0,3), (1,2), (1,0), (3,1), (3,3)\n\nc12: (l,i) allowed pairs: (0,0), (3,3), (2,1), (2,0), (0,3), (1,3)\n\nThus we need to find all (i,j,k,l) combos satisfying all four.\n\nWe can enumerate possibilities for i and j from c9, then for each possible j find k from c10, then for each k find l from c11, then check l,i from c12.\n\nLet's enumerate systematically.\n\n**Step 1: enumerate i,j pairs**:\n\nList all (i,j) from c9:\n\n1. (i=0, j=2)\n2. (i=2, j=0)\n3. (i=0, j=3)\n4. (i=3, j=2)\n5. (i=2, j=1)\n6. (i=2, j=3)\n\nNow for each, find possible k from c10 given j.\n\nc10 allowed (j,k):\n- (2,3)\n- (0,0)\n- (1,1)\n- (2,2)\n- (1,3)\n- (3,0)\n\nThus for each j, possible k values:\n\n- If j=2: allowed k = 3 (from (2,3)) only? Also check if any other pair with j=2? The list includes (2,3) only. So k=3.\n\n- If j=0: allowed k = 0 (from (0,0)) only? Also any (j=0,k?) There's (0,0) only. So k=0.\n\n- If j=3: allowed k = 0 (from (3,0)) only? Also (1,3) has j=1, not j=3. So only k=0.\n\n- If j=1: allowed k = 1 (from (1,1)) only? Also (1,3) has j=1, k=3. So for j=1, k can be 1 or 3.\n\n- If j=2: we already covered.\n\n- If j=3: we covered.\n\nThus for each (i,j) we have:\n\n1. (i=0, j=2) => k=3 only.\n2. (i=2, j=0) => k=0 only.\n3. (i=0, j=3) => k=0 only.\n4. (i=3, j=2) => k=3 only.\n5. (i=2, j=1) => k=1 or 3.\n6. (i=2, j=3) => k=0 only.\n\nNow for each (i,j,k) we need to find possible l from c11 (k,l). c11 allowed (k,l) pairs:\n\n- (3,0)\n- (0,3)\n- (1,2)\n- (1,0)\n- (3,1)\n- (3,3)\n\nThus for each k, possible l values:\n\n- If k=3: l can be 0,1,3 (from (3,0), (3,1), (3,3)). Also (3,?) any other? (3,?) only those three.\n\n- If k=0: l can be 3 (from (0,3)). Only.\n\n- If k=1: l can be 2 (from (1,2)) or 0 (from (1,0)). So l=2 or 0.\n\n- If k=2: not present? Actually there is no (2,l) in c11. So k=2 is invalid.\n\nThus for each case, we need to consider l possibilities.\n\nNow after we have (i,j,k,l), we must satisfy c12: (l,i) allowed pairs: (0,0), (3,3), (2,1), (2,0), (0,3), (1,3).\n\nThus for each (i,l) we need to check if (l,i) is in that list. Actually c12 constraint is (l,i) allowed pairs: the pairs listed are (l,i) combos. So we need (l,i) to be one of those.\n\nThus we need to check each candidate.\n\nLet's enumerate each case.\n\n**Case 1:** (i=0, j=2, k=3). l possibilities: 0,1,3.\n\n- (i=0,l=0): Check (l,i) = (0,0) is allowed? Yes, (0,0) is in c12 list. So (0,0) allowed. So (i,j,k,l) = (0,2,3,0) is valid.\n\n- (i=0,l=1): (l,i) = (1,0). Is (1,0) allowed? c12 list includes (0,0), (3,3), (2,1), (2,0), (0,3), (1,3). (1,0) is not present. So invalid.\n\n- (i=0,l=3): (l,i) = (3,0). Is (3,0) allowed? Not in list. The list includes (0,3) but not (3,0). So invalid.\n\nThus only (0,2,3,0) from case 1.\n\n**Case 2:** (i=2, j=0, k=0). l possibilities: only l=3 (since k=0 => l=3). So (i=2,j=0,k=0,l=3). Check (l,i) = (3,2). Is (3,2) allowed? c12 list includes (0,0), (3,3), (2,1), (2,0), (0,3), (1,3). (3,2) not present. So invalid. So case 2 yields no solution.\n\n**Case 3:** (i=0, j=3, k=0). l=3. So (i=0,j=3,k=0,l=3). Check (l,i) = (3,0). Not allowed. So invalid.\n\n**Case 4:** (i=3, j=2, k=3). l possibilities: 0,1,3.\n\n- (i=3,l=0): (l,i) = (0,3). Is (0,3) allowed? Yes, c12 includes (0,3). So (3,2,3,0) valid.\n\n- (i=3,l=1): (1,3) allowed? c12 includes (1,3). Yes. So (3,2,3,1) valid.\n\n- (i=3,l=3): (3,3) allowed? Yes, c12 includes (3,3). So (3,2,3,3) valid.\n\nThus three solutions from case 4: (3,2,3,0), (3,2,3,1), (3,2,3,3).\n\n**Case 5:** (i=2, j=1, k=1 or 3). Let's branch.\n\n- Subcase 5a: k=1. Then l possibilities: from k=1 => l=2 or 0.\n\n-- (i=2,j=1,k=1,l=2). Check (l,i) = (2,2). Is (2,2) allowed? c12 list includes (0,0), (3,3), (2,1), (2,0), (0,3), (1,3). (2,2) not present. So invalid.\n\n-- (i=2,j=1,k=1,l=0). (l,i) = (0,2). Is (0,2) allowed? Not in c12 list. So invalid.\n\nThus k=1 yields no solutions.\n\n- Subcase 5b: k=3. Then l possibilities: 0,1,3.\n\n-- (i=2,j=1,k=3,l=0). (l,i) = (0,2). Not allowed.\n\n-- (i=2,j=1,k=3,l=1). (l,i) = (1,2). Not allowed.\n\n-- (i=2,j=1,k=3,l=3). (l,i) = (3,2). Not allowed.\n\nThus case 5 yields no solutions.\n\n**Case 6:** (i=2, j=3, k=0). l=3. So (i=2,j=3,k=0,l=3). Check (l,i) = (3,2). Not allowed. So invalid.\n\nThus the only valid (i,j,k,l) combos are:\n\n1. (0,2,3,0)\n2. (3,2,3,0)\n3. (3,2,3,1)\n4. (3,2,3,3)\n\nSo we have 4 possible tuples for c3.\n\nBut we need to ensure these can be extended to a full solution. There may be further constraints linking i to f (c18) and j to m (c19). So we need to see if for each of these tuples, we can assign values to other variables satisfying all constraints.\n\nThus we need to find assignments for a,b,c,d,e,f,g,h,m,n,o,p such that:\n\n- (i,j,k,l) is one of the above.\n- c18: (f,i) allowed pairs: (2,0), (3,1). So (f,i) must be either (2,0) or (3,1). So for each i, we need to see possible f values.\n\n- c19: (j,m) allowed pairs: (2,3), (0,0). So (j,m) must be either (2,3) or (0,0). So for each j, possible m.\n\n- c17: (b,e) allowed pairs: (0,2), (0,3). So b must be 0 and e must be 2 or 3.\n\n- c20: (n,a) allowed pairs: (2,1), (3,3). So (n,a) must be either (2,1) or (3,3).\n\nThus we need to find assignments for a,b,c,d,e,f,g,h,m,n,o,p that satisfy all constraints in cycles c1-c4, c5-c8, c13-c16, plus cross constraints.\n\nGiven the limited possibilities for b,e and n,a, we can try to solve.\n\nLet's treat each cycle separately.\n\n### Cycle a,b,c,d (c1-c4)\n\nVariables: a,b,c,d.\n\nConstraints:\n- c1: (a,b) allowed pairs: (1,0), (1,3), (0,2), (2,3), (0,1), (0,3)\n- c2: (b,c) allowed pairs: (0,3), (1,1), (1,3), (1,2), (3,3), (0,2)\n- c3: (c,d) allowed pairs: (3,1), (0,1), (0,2), (1,0), (1,3), (1,1)\n- c4: (d,a) allowed pairs: (1,1), (2,1), (1,2), (2,2), (2,0), (3,2)\n\nWe also have cross constraints: c17: (b,e) allowed (0,2) or (0,3) => b=0, e=2 or 3. So b must be 0. Also c20: (n,a) allowed (2,1) or (3,3) => a must be 1 if n=2, or a=3 if n=3. So a is either 1 or 3.\n\nThus b=0 is forced by c17. Let's check if any other constraints allow b=0. c1 and c2 must be satisfied with b=0.\n\nNow c17 also forces e=2 or 3.\n\nThus we can start solving the a,b,c,d cycle with b=0, a∈{1,3} (due to n constraints). Let's see.\n\nGiven b=0, we need to find possible a from c1: allowed (a,b) pairs include (1,0), (0,2) but b=0, so possible pairs with b=0 are:\n\n- (a=1,b=0) is allowed.\n- (a=0,b=2) not applicable because b=0.\n- (a=2,b=3) no.\n- (a=0,b=1) no.\n- (a=0,b=3) no.\n- (a=1,b=3) no.\n\nThus only (a=1,b=0) is allowed. So a must be 1. That matches c20: (n,a) allowed (2,1) or (3,3). So a=1 is allowed only with n=2. So n must be 2.\n\nThus a=1, b=0, n=2.\n\nThus c20 satisfied: (n=2,a=1) is allowed (2,1) pair.\n\nThus we have a=1, b=0, n=2.\n\nNow we need to find c,d satisfying c2 and c3 and c4.\n\nGiven b=0, c2: (b,c) allowed pairs: (0,3) and (0,2) are the only ones with b=0? Let's list c2 allowed pairs: (0,3), (1,1), (1,3), (1,2), (3,3), (0,2). So with b=0, possible c values: c=3 (pair (0,3)) or c=2 (pair (0,2)). So c ∈ {2,3}.\n\nNow c3: (c,d) allowed pairs: (3,1), (0,1), (0,2), (1,0), (1,3), (1,1). So for each possible c, we need to find d.\n\n- If c=2: does any allowed pair have c=2? Let's see: (0,2) has c=0, not 2. (1,2) not present. (2,?) none. Actually c3 list includes (0,2) meaning (c=0,d=2). No pair with c=2. So c cannot be 2. So c must be 3.\n\nThus c=3.\n\nNow c3 with c=3: allowed pairs where c=3? c3 list: (3,1) is (c=3,d=1). That's the only one with c=3. So d=1.\n\nThus d=1.\n\nNow check c4: (d,a) allowed pairs: (1,1), (2,1), (1,2), (2,2), (2,0), (3,2). Here d=1, a=1 => (1,1) is allowed. So c4 satisfied.\n\nThus a,b,c,d solved uniquely: a=1,b=0,c=3,d=1.\n\nThus the a,b,c,d cycle is uniquely determined given cross constraints.\n\nThus we have a=1,b=0,c=3,d=1,n=2.\n\nNow we have constraints for e,f,g,h (c5-c8) with cross constraints c17 (b,e) and c8 (h,e) and c5 (e,f), c6 (f,g), c7 (g,h). Also c18 (f,i) linking to i.\n\nWe also have constraints for m,n,o,p (c13-c16) with cross constraints c19 (j,m) and c20 (n,a) already satisfied, c16 (p,m) linking to m, etc.\n\nThus we need to solve e,f,g,h cycle and m,n,o,p cycle.\n\nLet's start with e,f,g,h.\n\n### Variables e,f,g,h\n\nConstraints:\n\n- c5: (e,f) allowed pairs: (2,2), (1,2), (3,1), (1,3), (1,0), (2,0)\n- c6: (f,g) allowed pairs: (2,2), (1,1), (0,0), (1,3), (0,2), (0,3)\n- c7: (g,h) allowed pairs: (2,0), (3,2), (1,1), (2,1), (1,3), (3,1)\n- c8: (h,e) allowed pairs: (0,2), (1,0), (1,3), (2,3), (3,0), (2,1)\n\nCross constraints:\n\n- c17: (b,e) allowed pairs: (0,2), (0,3). Since b=0, e must be 2 or 3.\n\nThus e ∈ {2,3}.\n\nAlso c18: (f,i) allowed pairs: (2,0), (3,1). So for each i (from c3 tuple), f must be either 2 if i=0, or 3 if i=1. Since i can be 0 or 3 (looking at our c3 solutions: i values are 0 (first tuple) or 3 (others)). So:\n\n- If i=0 => f must be 2 (since (2,0) is allowed).\n- If i=3 => f must be 3 (since (3,1) is allowed). Note (3,1) means f=3,i=1? Wait c18 allowed pairs: (2,0) and (3,1). The pair is (f,i). So (f=2,i=0) or (f=3,i=1). So if i=0, f=2. If i=1, f=3. But i values are 0 or 3. So i=0 => f=2. i=3 => no allowed pair because (f,i) must be (2,0) or (3,1). There's no (f,i) with i=3. So any solution with i=3 is impossible because c18 cannot be satisfied. Wait check: c18 allowed pairs: (2,0) and (3,1). So i must be 0 or 1. But our c3 solutions have i values: 0,3,3,3. So only the tuple with i=0 is possible: (0,2,3,0). That has i=0. The other three tuples have i=3, which cannot satisfy c18. Therefore only the first tuple (i=0,j=2,k=3,l=0) can be part of a full solution. So we can prune other three c3 tuples.\n\nThus the only viable c3 tuple is (i=0, j=2, k=3, l=0). So we will have i=0, j=2, k=3, l=0.\n\nThus we have cross constraints:\n\n- c18: (f,i) => (f,0) must be (2,0) => f=2.\n\n- c19: (j,m) => (2,m) must be either (2,3) or (0,0). Since j=2, allowed pairs: (2,3) or (0,0). So (j,m) = (2,3) => m=3. Or (j,m) = (0,0) is impossible because j=2 not 0. So m must be 3.\n\nThus m=3.\n\nThus we have determined f=2, m=3.\n\nNow we need to solve e,f,g,h cycle with e ∈ {2,3} (from c17) and f=2 (from above). So e must be such that (e,f) allowed in c5: (e,2) must be in allowed pairs for c5.\n\nc5 allowed (e,f): (2,2), (1,2), (3,1), (1,3), (1,0), (2,0). So (e,f) pairs where f=2 are:\n\n- (2,2) allowed: e=2, f=2.\n- (1,2) allowed: e=1, f=2.\n- (2,0) has f=0, not relevant.\n- (3,1) f=1.\n- (1,3) f=3.\n- (1,0) f=0.\n\nThus with f=2, possible e values are 2 (via (2,2)) or 1 (via (1,2)). But e must be 2 or 3 from c17. So e=2 is allowed; e=1 is not allowed. So e must be 2.\n\nThus e=2.\n\nNow we have e=2, f=2.\n\nNow c6: (f,g) => (2,g) must be allowed. c6 allowed pairs: (2,2), (1,1), (0,0), (1,3), (0,2), (0,3). So with f=2, possible g values: g=2 (pair (2,2)). Also any other? No, only (2,2). So g=2.\n\nThus g=2.\n\nNow c7: (g,h) => (2,h) must be allowed. c7 allowed pairs: (2,0), (3,2), (1,1), (2,1), (1,3), (3,1). So with g=2, possible h values: h=0 (pair (2,0)) or h=1 (pair (2,1)). So h ∈ {0,1}.\n\nNow c8: (h,e) => (h,2) must be allowed. c8 allowed pairs: (0,2), (1,0), (1,3), (2,3), (3,0), (2,1). So we need (h,2) to be one of those. Let's list allowed (h,e) pairs:\n\n- (0,2) => h=0, e=2 allowed.\n- (1,0) => h=1, e=0 not relevant (e=2).\n- (1,3) => h=1, e=3 not relevant.\n- (2,3) => h=2, e=3 not relevant.\n- (3,0) => h=3, e=0 not relevant.\n- (2,1) => h=2, e=1 not relevant.\n\nThus only (0,2) works for e=2. So h must be 0.\n\nThus h=0.\n\nThus we have e=2,f=2,g=2,h=0.\n\nNow check all constraints for e,f,g,h:\n\n- c5 (e,f) = (2,2) allowed.\n- c6 (f,g) = (2,2) allowed.\n- c7 (g,h) = (2,0) allowed.\n- c8 (h,e) = (0,2) allowed.\n- c17 (b,e) = (0,2) allowed.\n\nThus e,f,g,h cycle solved uniquely: e=2,f=2,g=2,h=0.\n\nThus we have solved all variables except m,n,o,p.\n\nWe have determined:\n\n- a=1\n- b=0\n- c=3\n- d=1\n- e=2\n- f=2\n- g=2\n- h=0\n- i=0\n- j=2\n- k=3\n- l=0\n- m=3 (from j,m)\n- n=2 (from a,n)\n- o and p unknown (to be solved)\n- p unknown\n\nNow we need to solve m,n,o,p cycle (c13-c16) with constraints:\n\nc13: (m,n) allowed pairs: (3,2), (2,2), (3,3), (1,1), (0,2), (1,2)\nc14: (n,o) allowed pairs: (2,3), (3,2), (1,0), (1,2), (0,3), (2,2)\nc15: (o,p) allowed pairs: (3,3), (1,0), (0,0), (0,1), (1,3), (3,2)\nc16: (p,m) allowed pairs: (3,3), (1,0), (0,2), (1,1), (0,1), (3,0)\n\nWe have m=3, n=2 (from earlier). Let's verify that (m,n) = (3,2) is allowed: c13 includes (3,2) yes. So (m=3,n=2) satisfies c13.\n\nNow we need to find o and p such that:\n\n- c14: (n,o) = (2,o) must be allowed. c14 allowed pairs: (2,3), (3,2), (1,0), (1,2), (0,3), (2,2). So with n=2, allowed o values: o=3 (pair (2,3)), or o=2 (pair (2,2)). So o ∈ {2,3}.\n\n- c15: (o,p) must be allowed.\n\n- c16: (p,m) = (p,3) must be allowed. c16 allowed pairs: (3,3), (1,0), (0,2), (1,1), (0,1), (3,0). So with m=3, we need (p,3) to be one of those. Let's list allowed (p,m) pairs:\n\n-- (3,3): p=3,m=3 allowed.\n-- (1,0): p=1,m=0 not relevant.\n-- (0,2): p=0,m=2 not relevant.\n-- (1,1): p=1,m=1 not relevant.\n-- (0,1): p=0,m=1 not relevant.\n-- (3,0): p=3,m=0 not relevant.\n\nThus only (p=3,m=3) works for m=3. So p must be 3.\n\nThus p=3.\n\nNow c16 satisfied: (p,m) = (3,3) is allowed.\n\nNow c15: (o,p) = (o,3) must be allowed. c15 allowed pairs: (3,3), (1,0), (0,0), (0,1), (1,3), (3,2). So (o,3) allowed if:\n\n- (3,3) => o=3.\n- (1,3) => o=1.\n- (3,2) => o=3? Actually (3,2) is (o=3,p=2) not relevant.\n- (0,1) => p=1 not relevant.\n- (0,0) => p=0 not relevant.\n- (1,0) => p=0 not relevant.\n\nThus allowed (o,3) combos: o=3 (via (3,3)), or o=1 (via (1,3)). So o ∈ {1,3}.\n\nBut earlier from c14, o ∈ {2,3}. Intersection yields o=3 only (since 1 not allowed). So o=3.\n\nThus o=3.\n\nNow check c14: (n,o) = (2,3) is allowed (c14 includes (2,3)). Good.\n\nNow c15: (o,p) = (3,3) is allowed (c15 includes (3,3)). Good.\n\nThus the m,n,o,p cycle solved uniquely: m=3,n=2,o=3,p=3.\n\nThus we have a full assignment for all variables:\n\n- a=1\n- b=0\n- c=3\n- d=1\n- e=2\n- f=2\n- g=2\n- h=0\n- i=0\n- j=2\n- k=3\n- l=0\n- m=3\n- n=2\n- o=3\n- p=3\n\nWe need to verify all constraints are satisfied. Let's double-check each constraint:\n\nc1 (a,b) = (1,0) allowed? Yes (1,0) is in c1 allowed pairs.\n\nc2 (b,c) = (0,3) allowed? Yes (0,3) is in c2.\n\nc3 (c,d) = (3,1) allowed? Yes (3,1) is in c3.\n\nc4 (d,a) = (1,1) allowed? Yes (1,1) is in c4.\n\nc5 (e,f) = (2,2) allowed? Yes (2,2) is in c5.\n\nc6 (f,g) = (2,2) allowed? Yes (2,2) is in c6.\n\nc7 (g,h) = (2,0) allowed? Yes (2,0) is in c7.\n\nc8 (h,e) = (0,2) allowed? Yes (0,2) is in c8.\n\nc9 (i,j) = (0,2) allowed? Yes (0,2) is in c9.\n\nc10 (j,k) = (2,3) allowed? Yes (2,3) is in c10.\n\nc11 (k,l) = (3,0) allowed? Yes (3,0) is in c11.\n\nc12 (l,i) = (0,0) allowed? Yes (0,0) is in c12.\n\nc13 (m,n) = (3,2) allowed? Yes (3,2) is in c13.\n\nc14 (n,o) = (2,3) allowed? Yes (2,3) is in c14.\n\nc15 (o,p) = (3,3) allowed? Yes (3,3) is in c15.\n\nc16 (p,m) = (3,3) allowed? Yes (3,3) is in c16.\n\nc17 (b,e) = (0,2) allowed? Yes (0,2) is in c17.\n\nc18 (f,i) = (2,0) allowed? Yes (2,0) is in c18.\n\nc19 (j,m) = (2,3) allowed? Yes (2,3) is in c19.\n\nc20 (n,a) = (2,1) allowed? Yes (2,1) is in c20.\n\nAll constraints satisfied. So we have at least one full solution.\n\nNow we need to enumerate all satisfying assignments for component c3 (i,j,k,l). We found only one tuple (0,2,3,0) appears in this solution. But there might be other full solutions with different assignments for other variables that still satisfy constraints but produce other c3 tuples. However earlier we reasoned that only c3 tuple (0,2,3,0) is possible because c18 restricts i to 0 or 1. Our c3 tuple possibilities included i=0 and i=3. Since c18 only allows i=0 (with f=2) or i=1 (with f=3). There is no i=3 allowed. So any solution must have i=0 (since i=0 is allowed) or i=1 (but we have no tuple with i=1). So i must be 0. Thus only c3 tuple with i=0 is viable. That is (0,2,3,0). So indeed only that tuple can appear in any solution.\n\nBut we need to be thorough: Are there any other c3 tuples with i=0? Our enumeration gave only (0,2,3,0). Let's double-check enumeration for c3 to ensure we didn't miss any other (i,j,k,l) combos with i=0.\n\nWe enumerated all possible (i,j) from c9. For i=0, possible j values: from c9 allowed pairs with i=0: (0,2) and (0,3). So i=0, j=2 or j=3.\n\nWe considered both:\n\n- i=0,j=2 => k=3 => l possibilities: 0,1,3 => only l=0 works (due to c12). So (0,2,3,0) is valid.\n\n- i=0,j=3 => k=0 => l=3 => (0,3,0,3) fails c12. So no other.\n\nThus only (0,2,3,0) for i=0.\n\nThus only one c3 tuple possible.\n\nThus the answer should be a JSON with \"tuples\": [[0,2,3,0]].\n\nBut we need to verify if there could be any alternative assignments for other variables that allow i=1? Let's check if any c3 tuple has i=1. Our enumeration didn't include any because c9 allowed i values: from c9 allowed pairs: (0,2), (2,0), (0,3), (3,2), (2,1), (2,3). So i can be 0,2,3. No i=1. So i cannot be 1. So c18 cannot be satisfied if i=1, but there is no i=1 anyway. So i must be 0 or 2 or 3. But c18 only allows i=0 (with f=2) or i=1 (with f=3). So i=2 or 3 are not allowed. Therefore only i=0 is possible. So indeed only the tuple with i=0 is viable.\n\nThus the only c3 tuple is (0,2,3,0). So the answer is just that tuple.\n\nBut we need to double-check that there is no alternative assignment for other variables that could allow i=0 but with different j,k,l? Our enumeration for c3 gave only one tuple with i=0. So no alternative.\n\nThus answer is a JSON with \"tuples\": [[0,2,3,0]].\n\nBut we need to ensure the JSON format matches the template. The template includes fields:\n\n{\n  \"intent\": \"PROPOSE\",\n  \"message_id\": \"csp-6412-c3-proposer-1\",\n  \"payload\": {\n    \"claim\": {\n      \"claim_id\": \"c3-tuples\",\n      \"dependencies\": [\n        {\n          \"claim_revision\": 1,\n          \"resource_id\": \"problem:csp-6412\"\n        }\n      ],\n      \"provenance\": {\n        \"message_id\": \"csp-6412-c3-proposer-1\"\n      },\n      \"revision\": 1,\n      \"state_revision\": 1,\n      \"status\": \"active\",\n      \"value\": {\n        \"component\": \"c3\",\n        \"tuples\": [\n          [\n            0,\n            0,\n            0,\n            0\n          ]\n        ],\n        \"variables\": [\n          \"i\",\n          \"j\",\n          \"k\",\n          \"l\"\n        ]\n      }\n    }\n  },\n  \"problem_id\": \"csp-6412\",\n  \"read_refs\": [\n    {\n      \"resource_id\": \"problem:csp-6412\",\n      \"revision\": 1,\n      \"state_revision\": 1\n    },\n    {\n      \"resource_id\": \"claim:c3-tuples\",\n      \"revision\": 0,\n      \"state_revision\": 0\n    }\n  ],\n  \"recipients\": [\n    \"c2-reviewer\",\n    \"shared-integrator\"\n  ],\n  \"reply_to\": null,\n  \"schema_version\": 1,\n  \"sender\": \"c3-proposer\"\n}\n\nWe need to edit only payload.claim.value.tuples. So we need to replace the placeholder tuple with the correct tuple(s). The \"tuples\" array should contain all satisfying tuples for component c3. Since we have only